<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="reed-quadratic-derivation">
  <title>Where the quadratic formula comes from</title>
  <p>The formula is the result of one reusable idea: <term>completing the
  square</term>. We give a visual example of this below and then show how
  the quadratic formula can be derived.</p>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Visual example of completing the square</title>
    <p>Consider <m>x^2+6x=7</m>. For the picture, temporarily suppose
    <m>x&gt;0</m> so it represents a length. Draw a square of area
    <m>x^2</m>, and split the rectangle of area <m>6x</m> into two
    strips, each with area <m>3x</m>. Place one strip on the right and
    one below the square. A <m>3</m>-by-<m>3</m> corner is missing.</p>
    <figure xml:id="reed-quadratic-square-figure">
      <caption>Split the linear term in half, then fill the missing corner.
      Adding area <m>9</m> creates a square with side <m>x+3</m>.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/quadratic-completing-square.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Two area diagrams. The first has a square labeled
        x squared with two adjacent strips labeled 3x and a dashed missing
        3 by 3 corner. The second fills the corner with area 9, making a
        square of side x+3. Below: x squared plus 6x plus 9 equals (x+3) squared.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>The equation balances two quantities. Add <m>9</m> to
    <em>both</em> sides, not just to the picture:</p>
    <p><md>
      <mrow>x^2+6x&amp;=7,</mrow>
      <mrow>x^2+6x+9&amp;=7+9,</mrow>
      <mrow>(x+3)^2&amp;=16,</mrow>
      <mrow>x+3&amp;=\pm4,</mrow>
      <mrow>x&amp;=1\quad\text{or}\quad x=-7.</mrow>
    </md></p>
    <p>Although negative <m>x</m> cannot serve as a side length in this
    picture, the identity <m>x^2+6x+9=(x+3)^2</m> holds for every real
    <m>x</m>. Both algebraic solutions are valid:
    <m>1+6=7</m> and <m>49-42=7</m>. The diagram motivates the
    identity; the algebra is what extends it to all inputs.</p>
    <p>For a general linear coefficient <m>p</m>, half of it is
    <m>p/2</m> and the missing corner has square <m>(p/2)^2</m>:</p>
    <p><md>x^2+px+\left(\frac p2\right)^2
    =\left(x+\frac p2\right)^2.</md></p>
    <p>Expanding the right side verifies this even when <m>p</m> or
    <m>x</m> is negative, so no assumption about positive lengths is
    needed in the derivation below.</p>
  </paragraphs>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Quadratic formula derivation</title>
    <p>Start with <m>ax^2+bx+c=0</m> and <m>a\ne0</m>. The steps
    work for positive or negative <m>a</m>.</p>
    <p><ol>
      <li><p><em>Divide by <m>a</m> and move the constant.</em> A leading
      coefficient of <m>1</m> lets us use the square identity:</p>
      <p><md>x^2+\frac ba x=-\frac ca.</md></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Halve the coefficient of <m>x</m>, square it, and add
      to both sides.</em> Here the half-coefficient is <m>b/(2a)</m>:</p>
      <p><md>x^2+\frac ba x+\frac{b^2}{4a^2}
      =-\frac ca+\frac{b^2}{4a^2}.</md></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Write the left side as a square and combine the right
      side over a common denominator.</em> Since
      <m>-c/a=-4ac/(4a^2)</m>,</p>
      <p><md>\left(x+\frac{b}{2a}\right)^2
      =\frac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2}.</md></p>
      <p>This explains the discriminant: <m>b^2-4ac</m> appears when
      we combine the two terms on the right.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Multiply both sides by <m>4a^2</m>.</em> This avoids
      having to simplify <m>\sqrt{a^2}=|a|</m>:</p>
      <p><md>(2ax+b)^2=b^2-4ac.</md></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Take both square-root branches, then solve for
      <m>x</m>.</em> When <m>D=b^2-4ac\ge0</m>,</p>
      <p><md>
        <mrow>2ax+b&amp;=\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac},</mrow>
        <mrow>2ax&amp;=-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac},</mrow>
        <mrow>x&amp;=\boxed{\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}.}</mrow>
      </md></p>
      <p>If <m>D&lt;0</m>, a real square cannot equal <m>D</m>, so
      there are no real solutions. Allowing complex square roots gives
      the same formula for the two complex solutions.</p></li>
    </ol></p>
    <p>Every step preserves the solutions: we divide only by the nonzero
    numbers <m>a</m> and <m>2a</m>, add equal quantities to both sides,
    and keep both possibilities when undoing a square. The symbol
    <m>\pm</m> is essential because both a positive number and its
    negative have the same square.</p>
  </paragraphs>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Graphical interpretation of completing the square</title>
    <p>The completed-square equation also says</p>
    <p><md>\left(x-\left(-\frac b{2a}\right)\right)^2
    =\frac{D}{4a^2}.</md></p>
    <p>If <m>D&gt;0</m>, the two real roots lie the same horizontal
    distance from the symmetry line <m>x=-b/(2a)</m>. That distance
    is <m>\sqrt{D}/(2|a|)</m>. For the function in our first page,
    <m>f(x)=x^2-2x-3=(x-1)^2-4</m>, the equation <m>f(x)=0</m>
    becomes <m>(x-1)^2=4</m>. The roots are two units to either side
    of <m>x=1</m>.</p>
    <figure xml:id="reed-quadratic-root-distance">
      <caption>The two signs in the formula locate roots on opposite sides
      of the symmetry line. Here <m>x=1\pm2</m> gives <m>-1</m> and
      <m>3</m>.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/quadratic-root-distance.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Graph of (x-1) squared minus 4 with vertex
        (1,-4), symmetry line x=1, and roots -1 and 3. Two arrows show a
        horizontal distance of 2 from the symmetry line to each root.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>If <m>D=0</m>, that distance is zero: the two branches coincide
    at the vertex. If <m>D&lt;0</m>, there is no real distance with that
    square, and the graph has no x-intercept. This is why the formula,
    factoring, and the graph all tell the same story.</p>
  </paragraphs>
  <example xml:id="reed-quadratic-example-negative-linear">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>x^2-6x+2=0</m> by completing the square,
    then compare with the quadratic formula.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>Move the constant to obtain <m>x^2-6x=-2</m>.
    Half of <m>-6</m> is <m>-3</m>; its square is <m>9</m>.
    Add <m>9</m> to both sides:</p>
    <p><md>
      <mrow>x^2-6x+9&amp;=-2+9,</mrow>
      <mrow>(x-3)^2&amp;=7,</mrow>
      <mrow>x&amp;=3\pm\sqrt{7}.</mrow>
    </md></p>
    <p>The formula agrees: <m>D=(-6)^2-4(1)(2)=28</m> and
    <m>x=(6\pm\sqrt{28})/2=3\pm\sqrt{7}</m>.
    Completing the square uses the same steps that produced the formula.</p></solution>
  </example>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-complete-check">
    <statement><p>What must be added to <m>x^2-10x</m> to complete
    the square? Use it to solve <m>x^2-10x+13=0</m>. Explain why
    you add the same number to both sides and why you need two signs.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>Half of <m>-10</m> is <m>-5</m>, whose square is
    <m>25</m>. Starting with <m>x^2-10x=-13</m>, add <m>25</m>
    to both sides to preserve equality. This gives
    <m>(x-5)^2=12</m>, hence <m>x=5\pm2\sqrt{3}</m>.
    Both signs are needed because both <m>2\sqrt{3}</m> and
    <m>-2\sqrt{3}</m> square to <m>12</m>.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <p>Return to <xref ref="reed-quadratic-solving" text="title"/> for
  practice, or to the <xref ref="reed-quadratic-functions" text="title"/>
  page for the lesson overview.</p>
</section>
